            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Background: Why Does Agency Account Opening Always Get "Stuck"?</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team expanding into Southeast Asia was simultaneously working with agents for Google, Meta, and TikTok. Due to lack of unified management tools, account opening status was scattered across WeChat, emails, and spreadsheets. Operations staff spent hours daily chasing progress, and month-end reconciliation revealed mismatched prepayment details across multiple accounts.</p>
<p>This scenario is not uncommon. Agency account opening involves multiple stages: document submission, qualification review, account creation, and top-up configuration. Without systematic management, three major pain points emerge: opaque status, redundant work, and risk vulnerabilities.</p>
<p>Kanban management visualizes the entire process, making it clear at a glance "what's at which stage, who's responsible, and what comes next."</p>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 1: Build Kanban Infrastructure</h2>
<h3>1.1 Choose Your Kanban Tool</h3>
<p>Recommended tools: Notion, Trello, or Feishu Multi-dimensional Tables. Core requirements:</p>
<ul>
<li>Supports real-time multi-user collaboration</li>
<li>Customizable fields (status, owner, platform, region, etc.)</li>
<li>Supports filtering and view switching</li>
<li>Mobile app available for on-the-go updates</li>
</ul>
<h3>1.2 Define Standard Columns (Swimlanes)</h3>
<p>Divide the account opening process into 5-6 standard stages, each corresponding to one column:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Column Name</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
<th>Trigger</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Pending Start</td>
<td>Client submitted request, awaiting assignment</td>
<td>Received application</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Documents Preparing</td>
<td>Operations supplementing materials</td>
<td>Task claimed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Submitted for Review</td>
<td>Materials sent to agent</td>
<td>Submission confirmed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Under Review</td>
<td>Platform-side review stage</td>
<td>Agent confirmed receipt</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account Creating</td>
<td>Approved, agent creating account</td>
<td>Creation notification received</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Completed/Exception</td>
<td>Account delivered or process interrupted</td>
<td>Account live or needs resubmission</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>1.3 Set Key Fields</h3>
<p>Each card (task) must include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Client Name</strong>: Internal code to protect privacy</li>
<li><strong>Ad Platform</strong>: Google / Meta / TikTok / Other</li>
<li><strong>Target Region</strong>: US / Southeast Asia / Europe, etc.</li>
<li><strong>Account Type</strong>: Direct or Agency</li>
<li><strong>Prepayment Amount</strong>: For financial tracking</li>
<li><strong>Owner</strong>: Current stage contact person</li>
<li><strong>Due Date</strong>: SLA requirement</li>
<li><strong>Notes</strong>: Special requirements or exception details</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 2: Define Task Flow Rules</h2>
<h3>2.1 Trigger Mechanisms</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Auto-trigger</strong>: After completing a stage, the owner must immediately move the card to the next column</li>
<li><strong>Time Alert</strong>: Cards not updated in 48 hours are flagged red</li>
<li><strong>Notifications</strong>: Trello/Notion automation notifies the next-stage owner</li>
</ul>
<h3>2.2 Exception Handling</h3>
<p>If the process gets stuck (e.g., platform rejects the application):</p>
<ol>
<li>Move card to "Exception" column</li>
<li>Fill in rejection reason</li>
<li>Create sub-task: supplement materials / resubmit / contact client</li>
<li>After resolution, re-enter standard flow</li>
</ol>
<h3>2.3 Completion Criteria</h3>
<p>Each account opening task is complete when:</p>
<ul>
<li>Account created and accessible</li>
<li>Prepayment received</li>
<li>Account ID matches backend records</li>
<li>Documentation archived to cloud storage</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 3: Daily Operations Standards</h2>
<h3>3.1 Daily Must-Do (5 minutes)</h3>
<ol>
<li>Open kanban, check "Under Review" column task count</li>
<li>Check for cards exceeding SLA time</li>
<li>Update status of cards you're responsible for</li>
</ol>
<h3>3.2 Weekly Must-Do (30 minutes)</h3>
<ol>
<li>Count accounts opened this week</li>
<li>Analyze "Exception" column handling times</li>
<li>Archive completed cards older than 30 days</li>
</ol>
<h3>3.3 Monthly Review Points</h3>
<ul>
<li>Average opening cycle comparison across platforms</li>
<li>Exception reason classification (missing materials, qualification issues, agent delays, etc.)</li>
<li>Optimize process bottlenecks; communicate SLA improvements with agents when necessary</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Troubleshooting</h2>
<p><strong>Q: What if client changes target region mid-process?</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Mark original card as "Changed," duplicate to new card, modify region field, note "Changed from XXX." Avoid direct modification to preserve history.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Q: How to coordinate when multiple agents operate the same account?</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Record each communication's time, contact, and content in card notes. Single owner coordinates to avoid duplicate submissions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Q: What if top-up amount doesn't match account balance?</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Immediately create investigation task in "Exception" column. Contact agent to confirm top-up proof, provide resolution within 24 hours.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion: Core Value of Kanban Management</h2>
<p>The value of kanban management for agency account opening isn't about looking organized—it's about:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Centralized Information</strong>: One view shows all account statuses</li>
<li><strong>Clear Accountability</strong>: Each card has an owner, eliminating finger-pointing</li>
<li><strong>Visible Risks</strong>: Exception cards expose problems immediately</li>
<li><strong>Quantifiable Efficiency</strong>: Cycle statistics enable continuous optimization</li>
</ol>
<p>Recommendation: Run the process manually for 1-2 weeks first, then adjust column structure and rules based on actual experience. A systematic kanban isn't a set-and-forget tool—it's a management habit that requires continuous iteration.</p>
